A||||3||Richard Parnell||Clutch wear||||1||
Z||000000||Richard Parnell||09-25-2000||02:53 PM||||I was looking at the transmission throwout bearing and pressure plate set up on my car the other day.  I have it all apart right now for the V8 conversion.  The fork, bearing and pressure plate were completely worn and the reason I figured out was because in trying to get more throw from the clutch hydraulics I installed a longer rod on the slave .  This caused the fork to be continually pushing on the throwout bearing and in turn the bearing was constantly in contact with the pressure plate.  Therefore wearing the whole setup out over time.  We'll be making careful adjustments in the hydraulic set up to get maximum throw but still not wearing out parts through having them partially engaged.  The fellow helping me Bob Sato is a master mechanic and an expert in working on Fieros. He has several Fieros with V8s that he has built up.  Each has a different kit including one that he built all the parts himself and this (mine) project is his first Archie kit.  It should be running sometime next week. I can hardly wait to take it for the first spin.||||reg||1
Z||000001||StuGood||09-25-2000||05:39 PM||||Sounds like a pretty good source for data on the various kits.  Will be interesting to hear his thoughts, and yours,  after the Archie install.<P>Also - appreciate your observations on the clutch setup. I must ask - what kind of transaxle was it? Isuzu - ?<P>Thanks ||||reg||10
Z||000002||mshill||09-25-2000||05:46 PM||||Richard,  this is probably a good example of what happens when we try to fix the symptom of a problem on our cars rather than fixing the problem.  You tell me if I am wrong here, but the whole clutch system works properly if all the components are in good shape.  I am assuming you had a problem getting the proper travel out of the slave, hence the longer rod,  when in fact the issue was more likely to be the master or slave hydrolics weren't all up to par.  <P>I went through the whole clutch ordeal and found several problems, 1) leaky master seals... 2) insuffient bleeding... 3) worn clutch disk.  Once the leak was plugged and a new clutch installed, the system bled properly the first try and all is well. ||||reg||1
Z||000003||Richard Parnell||09-25-2000||07:03 PM||||Stugood-  The tranny was an Isuzu.  Don't know if it makes any difference tho.<BR>mshill- I think the problem is in getting matching master and slave cylinders.  By matching I mean cylinders with equal volumes of fluid capacity.  My problem was when my clutch started giving me problems I stuck in auto transmission fluid and it lasted about 2-3 months and completely deteriorated the seals.  Then I went and bought both master and slave and never could get things to work correctly.  The mechanic helping me has never had a problem with his trannies working right.  People bring their Fieros to him with the shifting problem and he sends them out the door as happy campers.  So he has it down and I am expecting no problems with the shifting gremlin.||||reg||1
